entries.append(proposal(id,[(r'Let $d\ge1$, let $K\subseteq C\subseteq\mathbb R^d$ with $K$ noncompact and $C$ convex, and let $\mathcal P$ be a nonempty family of Borel probability measures on $K$ sharing a prescribed barycenter.',r'Let $d\ge1$, let $K\subseteq C\subseteq\mathbb R^d$ with $K$ noncompact and $C$ convex, and let $\mathcal P$ be a nonempty family of Borel probability measures on $K$ sharing a prescribed barycenter. Precisely, for the coordinate functions $x_j:K\to\mathbb R$, every $P\in\mathcal P$ has finite first coordinate moments $\int_K|x_j|\,dP<\infty$ for $1\le j\le d$, and its barycenter is the vector $b(P):=(\int_Kx_1\,dP,\ldots,\int_Kx_d\,dP)\in\mathbb R^d$. Sharing the prescribed barycenter $\bar x$ means $b(P)=\bar x$ for every $P\in\mathcal P$.'),(r'Declare all Borel probability measures on $K$ with fixed barycenter $\bar x=0$ feasible, with entropy per mole $\int_Ks\,dP$.',r'Declare all Borel probability measures $P$ on $K$ with $\int_Kx\,dP=0$ feasible, with entropy per mole $\int_Ks\,dP$. Here $\int_K|x|\,dP\le1$ because $|x|<1$ on $K$, so the signed integral is defined and this is exactly the prescribed barycenter $\bar x=0$.' )]))

id='pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions'
entries.append(proposal(id,[(r'physical_scope: Finite coexisting phase branches in positive-temperature C2 charts at an attained global entropy maximum, and only the energy, volume, or species transfers declared pairwise feasible in both directions.',r'physical_scope: Finite coexisting phase branches in positive-temperature C2 charts at an attained global entropy maximum, and only pure pairwise energy, volume, or species transfers having feasible nonzero increments arbitrarily close to zero from each sign, with every other phase and auxiliary coordinate fixed.'),(r'For every pair of phases, assume the following about the feasible set at this endpoint: each named exchange channel permits a sufficiently small nonzero amount of transfer in both directions, while all other state coordinates may be held fixed.',r'For every pair of phases and each exchange channel used in the conclusion, assume the following about the exact feasible set at this endpoint. For every $\epsilon>0$ there is a feasible pure transfer with increment $a$ satisfying $0<a<\epsilon$, and there is one with increment $b$ satisfying $-\epsilon<b<0$, in the channel\'s fixed SI numerical coordinate. Each transfer adds its dimensioned increment to the selected coordinate of one phase and subtracts it from the same coordinate of the other phase, while every nonselected coordinate of both phases, every other phase, and every auxiliary state is fixed at its endpoint value. This requires feasible increments approaching zero from each sign; it does not require a full interval of feasible transfers.'),(r'and the bilateral local feasibility of each exchange direction used below.',r'and feasible pure increments arbitrarily close to zero from each sign in each exchange direction used below, with every nonselected phase and auxiliary coordinate fixed.'),(r'1.1 Fix any pair $\alpha,\beta$ and an allowed energy transfer. For any fixed nonzero increment $h$ joules, bilateral local feasibility supplies the curve $U_\alpha(t)=U_\alpha^*+th$, $U_\beta(t)=U_\beta^*-th$ for all sufficiently small $t$ of either sign, with all other coordinates fixed. The total entropy along this curve has a local maximum at $t=0$ by [A1]. Its derivative exists by [F2]; the right difference quotients are nonpositive and the left difference quotients are nonnegative, so the derivative is zero. By additivity and [L1], this derivative is $h(S_{\alpha,U}-S_{\beta,U})$. Since $h\ne0$, $S_{\alpha,U}=S_{\beta,U}$. [given, A1, F1, F2, L1, construct]',r'1.1 Fix any pair $\alpha,\beta$ and an allowed energy transfer, and take $h=1\,\mathrm J$. On a sufficiently small ambient interval define $U_\alpha(t)=U_\alpha^*+th$, $U_\beta(t)=U_\beta^*-th$ and let $f(t)$ be the sum of the two changing phase chart entropies and all fixed phase and auxiliary entropy contributions. The open phase charts and [F2] make $f$ differentiable at zero, whether or not every point of this interval is feasible. The stated feasibility condition provides nonzero feasible increments approaching zero from each sign. By [A1], $f(t)-f(0)\le0$ at those feasible points. The quotient $(f(t)-f(0))/t$ is therefore nonpositive on the positive feasible increments and nonnegative on the negative ones. Its unique derivative limit gives both $f\'(0)\le0$ and $f\'(0)\ge0$, hence $f\'(0)=0$. Additivity, the fixed auxiliary states and [L1] give $f\'(0)=h(S_{\alpha,U}-S_{\beta,U})$, so $S_{\alpha,U}=S_{\beta,U}$. [given, A1, F1, F2, L1, construct]'),(r'with both signs feasible. The same local-maximum quotient argument gives',r'with feasible values of $t$ arbitrarily close to zero from each sign and every other phase and auxiliary coordinate fixed. The same differentiability and feasible-quotient argument of step 1.1 gives'),(r'Both signs are feasible. The same quotient argument gives',r'Feasible values of $t$ approach zero from each sign, and every nonselected phase and auxiliary coordinate is fixed. The same differentiability and feasible-quotient argument of step 1.1 gives'),(r'only for the channels whose bilateral feasibility was assumed.',r'only for the channels whose arbitrarily small pure feasible transfers from both signs were assumed.'),(r'No derivative equality follows for a retained or boundary coordinate because its required two-sided feasible curve is absent.',r'No derivative equality is supplied for a retained coordinate, a one-sided boundary exchange, or an exchange admitting only isolated nonzero increments away from zero, because the required feasible increments approaching zero from each sign are absent. A whole interval of feasible transfers is sufficient but is not necessary.')]))

